if not backup.exists():
    backup.mkdir(parents=True)
    for name in ['storyboard_45min.json','storyboard_45min.html','SEMINAR_STORY_45_MIN.txt']:
        if (OUT/name).exists(): shutil.copy2(OUT/name,backup/name)

# title, minutes, takeaway, visual, speaker notes
cards=[
('Planning with the environment',1,'Build a reusable description of useful material-moving actions.','01_material',
 'Open with the physical collection task. Introduce the target and the five deposits. The mission objective remains open; do not promise time-optimal or multi-agent optimal planning.'),
('Should every pile go straight to the target?',3,'A push can deliver material or prepare it for a later collection.','01_material',
 'Keep the broom-and-dustpan intuition from old slides 3–4. Point to the small side pile and ask whether it should go directly upward or join the main line. Do not answer yet. This replaces switching between unrelated scenarios in old slides 22–24.'),
('Where this fits in the lab’s work',2,'The representation complements the question of how to form and transport aggregates.','',
 'Introduce Shaked et al., Aggregate-forming planner for autonomous earth-moving, IEEE Access, 2023. Use the existing thesis related-work source. Explain that the lab-inspired comparison is partial; do not label it a faithful reproduction of Tom’s complete planner. Return immediately to the running scene.'),
('Represent the scene as cells',2,'Each occupied cell carries a material count.','01_material',
 'Reveal the grid and counts: 5, 4, 3, 2 on the main line and 2 at the side; total 16. Counts are abstract material units, not calibrated kilograms. Coordinates are cell indices; the pictures place symbols at cell centers. The target is a polygon obtained from a buffered point.'),
('Which next steps are allowed?',3,'Visibility defines a directed graph of material-collecting moves.','02_successors',
 'Highlight source (8,3), then its target direction, the ±30° cone, nearer-to-target test and range gate. Reveal only successors returned by calculate_target_zone_visibility. The current implementation DOES apply a range gate, despite its outdated docstring. With no occupied successor, connect to a verified inside-target cell. These are geometric rules, not guarantees of rover feasibility.'),
('Find a collection route',3,'Search favors collected material, then shorter candidate routes.','03_routes',
 'Trace (8,3) → (8,7) → (8,11) → (8,15) → target. Show 5 → 9 → 12 → 14 collected. Explain reward-priority search, visibility-based heuristic, cycle rejection and the configured 2.5× distance cap. The terminal edge is charged to the target boundary, while the displayed polyline reaches an inside-target cell. Avoid calling this conventional shortest-path A* or claiming global optimality; per-cell reward pruning and suffix reuse need separate analysis.'),
('Reuse an already computed continuation',2,'Nearer cells can supply an onward route to farther cells.','03_routes',
 'Reveal the suffix from (8,11), then extend it upstream. The calculation processes occupied cells nearest to the target first. Explain that the junction count is subtracted when stitching. Solved flags are set by the no-spillage calculation; fresh spillage-enabled searches do not create them. Do not imply mode switching automatically clears old flags or that suffix reuse proves constrained optimality.'),
('From routes to values and headings',2,'Path value and direction provide the inputs to heat.','04_values',
 'Distinguish local count n(c), raw route yield and propagated value ψ(c). The main route gathers 14 even though no individual cell holds 14. Propagation takes a maximum over contributing selected paths; it does not add overlapping yields. The arrow is a normalized first-step direction, not speed. In spillage mode, selected route and maximum raw candidate yield can come from different candidates; reserve that subtlety for backup.'),
('Build the heatmap one contribution at a time',3,'Aligned downstream cells inherit an opportunity value.','05_heat',
 'Start with one arrow, then show its eligible contribution and finally the full max field. Use H(x)=max(w·Hown(x), max over eligible c of ψ(c)[max(0,v(c)·u(c,x))]^p). Eligibility includes a target-relative radial gate and first-child distance limit. Here w=0, p=1 and the radial gate is 75°. There is no distance-decay multiplier. Hown is the implementation’s propagated value at an occupied source, not necessarily its own raw yield. Heat is neither mass at x nor a time-saving prediction. Explain the cold upstream source: self contribution is disabled in this configuration.'),
('Return to the side pile',3,'A connector can stage material in the main collection corridor.','06_connector',
 'Reveal the actual connector (15,8) → (8,8). Its endpoint is empty before the push. Low-heat sources use anchors with at least 30% of maximum heat, within their distance-to-target radius; source threshold is 50%. Anchor score is 0.7·H/Hmax + 0.3·(1−distance/radius). Search then constructs a connector toward the selected anchor. Distinguish anchor scoring from candidate route ranking and from the later task scheduler. This example demonstrates an available action, not proof that the scheduler should choose it or that staging improves mission time.'),
('Estimate what survives a curved push',3,'The current estimator follows a smoothed route and deposits spilled material.','09_spillage',
 'Use the side pile’s target route from the same scene. First show waypoints, then the spline and its curvature. At each sample, collect previously unvisited material cells; proposed loss is min(load, coefficient × curvature × load), applied only above a threshold. Finally deposit the remainder and round the distribution. This actual small example predicts 4 of 4 at the endpoint: show that result honestly, not invented spill dots. Capacity is accepted as an argument but is not enforced by the current branch. Sampling and conservation correction limit the physical interpretation.'),
('Keep the estimator replaceable',2,'Candidate paths can be evaluated by another material-transport model.','',
 'Draw: waypoints + material state → estimator → predicted deposits + endpoint yield → candidate ranking / predicted state update. Preserve this contract when replacing curvature heuristics with a calibrated or learned model. Current USE_ADVISOR_MODEL selects a zero-spillage placeholder, not a validated second model. A replacement must define endpoint yield, off-path pickups, units, capacity and conservation; predictions also need comparison with observed rover outcomes. This is a replaceable building block, not a finished plugin system.'),
('Update the changed environment',4,'Reuse unaffected target routes, then refresh their downstream representation.','08_update',
 'First reveal the after-staging image: the side pile is empty and (8,8) contains 2. Then mark the three target-search sources (8,3), (8,7), (8,8). Direct changes plus visibility dependencies determine the selected set; solved flags are invalidated, visibility and routes refreshed, and values/headings propagated. Heat still scans the grid and candidate sources globally; connectors are reconsidered globally. This fixture uses 3 target searches versus 5 for a fresh state and matches its heat and target paths. That is one correctness example, not a timing result or universal equivalence proof.'),
('What the 2D benchmark can establish',3,'Separate fresh construction, cached queries and changed-state updates.','',
 'Use the existing historical operation chart, visibly labeled historical. Summarize the recovered cache issue in one sentence, with the detailed audit in backup. A clean comparison starts from identical post-action material and checks output agreement before timing full versus selective rebuilds. Measure visibility, search, propagation, heat and connectors separately. Do not state a verified 7–9× speedup or attribute the archived p-value to suffix reuse. The running example explains the mechanism; the historical benchmark does not isolate it.'),
('From the field to rover execution',3,'The planner supplies opportunities; execution must make the push happen.','',
 'Show one pipeline: observed material → grid → candidate action → approach and shovel alignment → path following and collision avoidance → refreshed observation. Add a rover clip only when the current run and task are verified. Mark CLIP PENDING in the working draft. Connect low-level control to deviations from predicted material movement; do not introduce a new unrelated scenario unless necessary.'),
('Time estimation is another foundation',2,'An execution-cost estimate can inform later action selection.','',
 'Explain approach, alignment and pushing as potential components. Keep the choice of objective open. Time estimation is not yet sufficiently validated to close the thesis with a time-optimal claim. If using T_q, define it only here as an experimental completion threshold; q is the chosen fraction of material delivered, not a heatmap parameter.'),
('Experiments that will close the story',3,'Test when the representation helps and where its predictions fail.','',
 'Keep explicit placeholders: matched full/selective timing and output checks; staging versus direct actions on controlled layouts; predicted versus observed retention; rover tracking/collision outcomes; timing accuracy if it remains relevant. Final primary metric and thesis conclusion are PENDING advisor discussion and evidence. Do not fill these placeholders with expected improvements.'),
('What is established, and what remains open',1,'The foundation connects material distribution, route generation, heat and execution.','07_after',
 'Return to the same scene after staging. State the implemented mechanism and demonstrated example. End with the open question: which action-selection criteria and experiments best establish its value? Leave final empirical conclusions pending.')]
